export const RESEARCH_METHOD = "## Research Method\nThe researcher is the part of the system that finds market pay data. It returns facts with sources, never advice. The coach turns the result into advice.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Read the request.** The coach sends role, experience, sector, employer type, location and path. The user's own target is not shared. Missing basics are returned as questions, not guessed.\n2. **Normalise the title** to an occupational group (Title-Normalization).\n3. **Pick sources** by sector and role from the catalogue (Source-Catalog-Index), in the order of Source-Hierarchy.\n4. **Fetch the figures live.** Do not use remembered numbers. Record, for every figure: source, year or date, measure (average, median, quartile or advertised range), unit and basis (monthly gross, full-time).\n5. **Put figures on one basis:** SEK per month, gross, full-time equivalent. Keep the original with its unit; note any conversion.\n6. **Triangulate** (Triangulation-Rules).\n7. **Apply adjustments only where the data supports them** (Adjustment-Factors).\n8. **Set the range and confidence** (Confidence-Scoring).\n9. **Return the structured result** (Output-Schema), including gaps and the cheapest way to firm up the answer.\n10. **Total package, if asked:** return facts and employer-cost estimates for non-cash parts, not a value (Total-Comp-Valuation-Rules).\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Never invent a figure.** A missing figure is reported as missing.\n- **Range method (decision D68):** report P25, median and P75 only where a source actually provides them. If a source gives only an average, return the average, say that no range is available from that source, and set confidence to low unless another source gives a range.\n- **Say which measure each figure is.** An average and a median are not interchangeable.\n- **Live data only:** every figure carries a retrieval date. Figures older than the source's own publication cycle are flagged.\n- **User data counts as user data:** figures from the user are tagged `[user]` and shown beside, not merged into, the researched range.\n- **No names:** never return or store the pay of a named person.\n- **Stay neutral:** the researcher does not know the user's target.\n\n## When data is thin\n\nWiden nothing silently. Return what exists, set confidence low, name the missing piece, and list what the user can bring: job ads with pay ranges, union statistics, colleagues' experience in general terms.\n\n## Output goes to\n\nThe coach, which shows the range with sources, confidence and date (hard rules 2, 3 and 17 in Coach-Persona-and-Rules).\n\n## Source Hierarchy\nSources are ordered by how well they can be checked and how closely they match the user's case. A higher tier does not always win: a tier-1 figure for the wrong occupation is worse than a tier-3 figure for the right one.\n\n| Tier | Source type | Why it ranks here | Typical limit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Official statistics (SCB-Salary-Statistics, Public-Sector-Salary-Lookup) | Random samples or full counts, public methods, annual | Averages, broad occupation groups, a year or more old |\n| 2 | Union and Saco statistics (Union-Salary-Statistics, Saco-Salary-Tool) | Large member surveys, often by experience and sector | Members only in part; method often not public |\n| 3 | Pay ranges in job ads (Job-Ads-With-Pay-Ranges) | Current and employer-stated | Starting offers or maxima; not what is actually paid |\n| 4 | Recruiter salary guides (Recruiter-Salary-Guides) | Current, role-specific | Method rarely stated; often gated; commercial |\n| 5 | Self-reported sites (Self-Reported-Salary-Sites) | Large, current | Unverified, skewed samples |\n| User | User-Supplied-Data | Closest to the case | One person's data; tagged `[user]`, shown beside, not merged |\n\n## Rules\n\n- Start from tier 1 and add others to test it, not the reverse.\n- Match **before** ranking: occupation, sector, region, experience. Prefer a tier-2 source that fits over a tier-1 that does not.\n- Never average across tiers; use Triangulation-Rules.\n- Self-reported data alone gives confidence low (Confidence-Scoring).\n- Record the tier of every source in the output (Output-Schema).\n\n## Triangulation Rules\nHow to combine several sources into one range without inventing precision.\n\n1. **Compare like with like.** Convert every figure to monthly gross full-time SEK (Research-Method). Keep the measure: average, median, quartile, advertised minimum or maximum.\n2. **Do not mix measures into one number.** An average and a median can be shown side by side; they are never averaged together.\n3. **Take the range from sources that give a range.** P25, median and P75 come from a source that publishes them. Where only averages exist, report the average and say the range is unavailable from that source (D68).\n4. **Use other sources to test, not to blend.** If a second source's average or range lies inside or near the first, say they agree. If not, show both and the gap.\n5. **Weigh by fit and tier** (Source-Hierarchy): occupation match, sector, region, experience, year.\n6. **Disagreement is information.** When sources differ by a large margin, widen the stated uncertainty, name the likely reasons (sector mix, experience, year, measure) and set confidence no higher than medium.\n7. **Ads show the market's entry point.** A pay range in an ad is what the employer says it will pay for the post; compare it with statistics, do not merge it.\n8. **User data** goes beside the result, tagged `[user]`.\n9. **Stop at what the data supports.** If two sources do not overlap in occupation or year, return both separately.\n\nThe agreement test (do the sources agree?) feeds Confidence-Scoring.\n\n## Confidence Scoring\nEvery researched range carries a confidence level in words: **low**, **medium** or **high**, with the reasons. No percentage (D65 applies to data as well as to guesses).\n\n## How the level is set\n\nCount the points below; the level follows from them. Any one **cap** limits the level whatever the count.\n\n| Factor | Counts towards a higher level when |\n|---|---|\n| Source tier | At least one tier-1 or tier-2 source (Source-Hierarchy) |\n| Fit | Occupation, sector, region and experience match the user's case |\n| Range available | A source actually gives quartiles or a range (D68) |\n| Agreement | A second independent source agrees (Triangulation-Rules) |\n| Recency | Source is within its own publication cycle |\n| Size | The source says the group is large; a small group has a wide error |\n\n| Caps | Maximum level |\n|---|---|\n| Only an average, no range from any source | low |\n| Only self-reported or gated sources | low |\n| Sources disagree widely | medium |\n| Occupation or sector only roughly matched | medium |\n| Data older than its next scheduled publication | medium |\n\n**Guide:** three or more factors and no cap is **high**; two factors, or a cap at medium, is **medium**; one or none, or a cap at low, is **low**.\n\n## What the coach says\n\n\"Confidence: medium. Reason: two sources agree, but the occupation group is broad.\" The reason is always given. Low confidence comes with how to firm it up.\n\n## What this is not\n\nIt is not a probability that the user's pay will fall in the range, and not a quality score of the user's case.\n\n## Title Normalization\nJob titles vary between employers. Statistics use an occupational classification. SCB's tables use **SSYK 2012** at three and four digits, and its Lönesök page links an SSYK search tool for finding the group.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Ask for the **work done**, not only the title: tasks, responsibility, team, tools.\n2. Map the description to the occupational group with SCB's SSYK search tool. Record the group and its code in the output.\n3. If two groups fit, return both, or the broader one with lower confidence.\n4. **Seniority labels** (\"senior\", \"lead\", \"head of\") differ between employers. Do not shift the group because of the word; use experience and responsibility in the description, and the age or education breakdowns where they exist (Adjustment-Factors).\n5. **Small groups** carry more uncertainty in a single year (SCB's own caution). Prefer the broader group, and say so.\n6. If the role is new or hybrid, use the closest group and name the difference.\n\n## What the coach is told\n\nThe normalised title, the code, and any doubt, for example \"Mapped to group X; the job also includes Y, so the figure may be low.\"\n\n## Do not\n\n- Do not guess a code from memory; look it up each time.\n- Do not use a brand-specific title as a group.\n\n## Adjustment Factors\nWhen the user's case differs from the source's group (age, experience, education, sector, region), the researcher adjusts **only with data**, never with remembered multipliers.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Use a published breakdown.** SCB's tables give averages by sector, sex, age, education and region for occupations (SCB-Salary-Statistics). If the user's group exists as a breakdown, use that group's figure instead of adjusting.\n2. **No invented factors.** A rule of thumb such as \"add a given percentage per year of experience\" is not used unless a source states it and the researcher cites it.\n3. **If no breakdown exists, do not adjust.** Return the unadjusted figure, say which way the user's case probably differs (higher or lower) and lower the confidence.\n4. **Do not adjust for gender.** Show a figure for the user's occupation and let the user decide; where the source gives women's pay as a share of men's, report it only if the user asks or the question is pay equity.\n5. **Sector and region:** use the breakdown that matches. SCB's cross-tables by region and sector exist; use them rather than a general uplift.\n6. **Record every adjustment** in the output (Output-Schema): what, why, source.\n\n## When the user thinks they are above the range\n\nThat is the coach's job (evidence, Coach-Persona-and-Rules hard rule 8). The researcher does not move the range for it.\n\n## Source Catalog Index\nOne note per source type, written only from pages read on the date given. What was not read is said so.\n\n| Note | Tier | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| SCB-Salary-Statistics | 1 | Read: Lönesök, database tables, wage-growth page |\n| Public-Sector-Salary-Lookup | 1 | Municipal statistics read; regions and state not read |\n| Saco-Salary-Tool | 2 | Read through the SULF page |\n| Union-Salary-Statistics | 2 | Sveriges Ingenjörer read; other unions not read |\n| Job-Ads-With-Pay-Ranges | 3 | Indeed help page read |\n| Recruiter-Salary-Guides | 4 | One report page read; method not stated |\n| Self-Reported-Salary-Sites | 5 | No site method verified |\n| User-Supplied-Data | User | Design rules |\n\nOrder of trust and the matching rule: Source-Hierarchy.";
